00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.930
So I know, whenever
people talk about tensors,

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:58.700
they typically will
talk about you,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.370
and I also know that many
people will constantly

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.680
ask the question, when
they hear about tensors,

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.330
do you mean TensorFlow?

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:06.980
Do you want to touch
on that briefly?

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:09.470
Tensors are in TensorFlow,
but that doesn't necessarily

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:10.204
mean TensorFlow.

00:07:10.204 --> 00:07:11.870
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I
mean, the good thing

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.670
about the deep
learning revolution

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:16.400
is now nobody is
afraid of tensors.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.920
Everybody has heard
of the word tensors,

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:22.610
whether it is TensorFlow as
a deep learning framework,

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.310
or tensor calls to do
the computations for deep

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.740
learning in Nvidia GPUs.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:31.040
But there, to me,
it's not completely

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.460
realizing the potential
of tensor computations.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:38.360
We still do matrix algebra
in these tensor calls,

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.280
or in our neural
network [INAUDIBLE],,

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.300
when you write a neural
network in TensorFlow,

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.400
so whereas you can expand
upon those operations

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.710
and think much more broadly.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:50.510
So what does TensorFlow means?

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:53.650
It really means you
have a flow of tensors

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.150
throughout these
layers of processing.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:57.920
The input is a tensor.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.770
Output in general
could be also a tensor,

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.760
and you have these intermediate
operations that transform it.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.310
And so right now, we are
doing these transformations

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.190
in somewhat simple
ways because designing

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:12.050
these architectures are hard.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:13.310
We are going by [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:15.380
We are saying, let's
do convolutions

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:19.280
because there is a
translation in variance,

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.630
and this is a useful thing to
have when we do [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.820
Let's then convert them
to fully connected layers

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:29.870
because I have to change these
three dimensional activations

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:31.850
of the convolutional
layer into something

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.280
I can process into
a category, which

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.960
is the output of a
classification task.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.929
So these are going
by simple intuitions,

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:41.250
but we can ask what
is the next step.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:44.960
Can we do much more
complex architectures,

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.590
but that can give us the benefit
of multiple dimensions, that

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:50.960
could potentially compress
our current networks

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:53.990
by large amounts, that could
help us generalize better

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:57.650
because it captures higher order
correlations in better and more

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.310
meaningful ways?

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:02.990
And that's where the tensor
algebra has the potential.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:05.540
As a simple example,
if you start

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.430
with these
convolutional networks,

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.380
like I was mentioning
before, you

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:11.810
have these three-dimensional
activations

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.270
when you go through the
convolutional layers

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:18.980
because you're looking at
how the filters are acting

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:22.370
on the input, and you have
these so many channels

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.260
that you accumulate
when you process

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:26.150
through all the filters.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:28.310
So you have these
three-dimensional shapes,

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:30.890
but when you come to
fully connected layers,

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:31.850
you vectorize it.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:34.640
You ignore all this
three dimensionality.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.710
You just treat it
as a vector, and you

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.230
do matrix multiplication with
the fully connected layer,

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.940
and you pass on the results
through few such layers

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.300
and, finally, get the output,
which is the classification.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:50.390
And so instead, we
asked, what if you'd

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.610
kept the three
dimensional information

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:53.920
all the way through?

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:56.000
Even in the fully
connected layer,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.280
you maintain the
three dimensionality.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:00.890
You contract the
tensor, which means

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:04.370
you're looking at three separate
weight matrices for the three

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.530
dimensions, and
you're learning them,

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:08.900
and when you're doing
regression in the last layer,

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:12.260
you're doing a low rank
parameterized weights.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:17.050
That means you can have a much
more compact set of parameters

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.380
that can give you
the same accuracy.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.060
In fact, we found more
than 65% space savings

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.750
in these fully connected
layers by incorporating

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:28.700
the multi-dimensionality.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:33.170
And what I have ambitions for
is to take it to the next level

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:36.320
and ask, suppose I can
realize entirely new

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.220
architectures by looking
at these higher order

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:41.940
tensor operations.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:45.650
What would they be, and what
kind of an impact can it make?

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.410
Especially if you think of
multi-modal information--

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:49.550
it's not just images.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:53.070
It's image and text
and other information.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.910
We have this explosion
of dimensionalities.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.970
Can we naturally encode
them with tensors

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.102
and carry it all the way
throughout the network.

00:11:01.102 --> 00:11:03.560
MELANIE WARRICK: And that's
the next level of your research

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.360
that you're currently
involved in,

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.170
in terms of what you're
going forward with.

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:08.930
So one of the other
