00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:40.550
Bryan Catanzaro, Jan Kautz,
and researchers from also

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:43.910
the computer graphics side,
architecture, hardware.

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:48.000
It's this interdisciplinary
nature of research here

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.330
and also the engineering
prowess and the scalability

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:54.350
that Nvidia offers.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:58.370
I'm very excited to think
of research problems

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:01.550
at the core of machine
learning, like the example of,

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:04.790
can we realize new neural
network architectures

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:07.670
but really realize
them at scale?

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:09.890
It's the full stack solution.

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:13.580
Can we think of better hardware,
better [INAUDIBLE] primitives,

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.790
better frameworks that make
it easy to design and realize

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.170
the algorithms?

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:21.140
And that's what makes
Nvidia so exciting to me,

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:23.520
because I can translate
that into practice.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.450
I can see the real
gains, but I'm also

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.630
given the freedom and a
team and the expertise

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.344
to ask how we can
realize those new dreams.

00:12:32.344 --> 00:12:34.010
MELANIE WARRICK: You
have this expertise

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:36.860
from the mathematical
and theoretical side

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:39.710
that you're also now able to
translate into this hardware

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.570
applied side of the world.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:44.120
It sounds pretty fantastic
to be able to marry the two.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.120
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Absolutely,
and Nvidia, to me,

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.474
is the best place to do that.

00:12:47.474 --> 00:12:48.890
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's wonderful.

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:52.430
Let me go ahead and shift gears
and talk a little bit about--

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:54.680
from the AI and the
machine learning space.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.110
I know you've been in the
academic field clearly

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:00.320
for years now, and the space
is well-known for having

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:02.270
some conferences that
are pretty established

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:04.970
for bringing together academics
to be able to share research,

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:06.080
to share knowledge.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:08.750
There's ones that stand out
as sort of the core ones,

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:10.340
although there's
a ton of them that

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:12.740
allow people to come together
for various specialties.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:14.960
Can you tell us a little
bit about those conferences,

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.769
why they matter so
much in this field?

00:13:16.769 --> 00:13:17.810
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Indeed.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:20.340
Back a few years ago--

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:24.170
well, going back when the
deep learning revolution

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:27.830
hadn't happened, then it
was these small academic

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:29.180
conferences.

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:33.570
For instance, having 700 or
800 was such a big number,

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.110
and most of them
were small workshops.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:39.680
And that was the only way
to disseminate information.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:42.110
You'd go actually
talk to the person

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.480
and get the full
understanding of the paper

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.435
during the poster
session, so you

00:13:46.435 --> 00:13:49.210
have these very deep
conversations there.

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:53.270
And so to me, since I didn't
start out my PhD in this field,

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:57.440
I kind of moved towards the end
of my PhD to machine learning,

00:13:57.440 --> 00:13:59.000
although the tools
I was using was

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:01.820
very relevent to solving
machine learning problems,

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:03.550
I found the community
so welcoming.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:05.690
I submitted the papers to NIPS--

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:07.400
now, what's called in NeurIPS--

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.120
and ICML and other conferences,
and I felt instantly

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.780
welcomed because there
was deep reviews.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:19.010
There was pages of very
deep technical discussions

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:23.240
during the review process,
and the community was small.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:27.050
It was well-knit, and we
made a lot of progress

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:31.365
in the conferences in terms
of intellectual engagements.

00:14:31.365 --> 00:14:32.990
MELANIE WARRICK: And
those have evolved

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:35.540
as neural nets, as machine
learning, have taken hold.

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:38.600
I've seen these conferences,
and as you mentioned, NIPS--

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:39.770
which is now NeurIPS--

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:43.220
has gone from, I guess it
was only a few hundred to--

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.500
what was it-- 8,000
not that long ago.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.250
I'm not sure what
it is this year.

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:48.416
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: It would--

00:14:48.416 --> 00:14:51.047
I mean, it could be much more
if there was no limit on--

00:14:51.047 --> 00:14:52.130
MELANIE WARRICK: Probably.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:53.555
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:53.555 --> 00:14:55.138
MELANIE WARRICK: So
part of the reason

00:14:55.138 --> 00:14:57.590
why I set this podcast up
was because I wanted us

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:01.160
specifically to dive into NIPS,
which the Neural Information

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:02.750
Processing Systems conference.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:05.420
Some out there, especially
those in the academic field,

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.250
may have heard about
the controversy that

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.750
played out this
year in particular

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:09.950
in terms of renaming it.

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:13.731
I think it sounds, just by us
saying the acronym, for many

00:15:13.731 --> 00:15:16.230
who have never heard of this
conference it might make sense.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:19.390
But unfortunately, it did
receive a lot of resistance.

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.220
Can you talk to
that a little bit,

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:25.730
about what transpired the naming
change, why this was an issue?

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:27.970
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: So
being in the community

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:31.780
before the deep learning
revolution, like I said,

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.540
it was a small,
well-knit community then.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:37.430
And the name, you know, you
think for it for a minute.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.030
But then you go
to the conference,

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:42.190
you have these
friendly discussions,

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:45.200
and you have such an
amazing experience,

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.710
then that's not
the primary focus.

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:51.250
I'm very proud of the
tradition that the conference

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:51.970
has spawned.

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.360
In fact, the conference
started out here at Caltech,

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:56.890
was founded here at Caltech.

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:58.380
Not many people know that.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:15:58.470
MELANIE WARRICK: No.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:15:59.928
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
And I'm extremely

00:15:59.928 --> 00:16:01.480
proud of that tradition.

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:06.100
And it was started by Professor
Yaser Mostafa and Ed Posner

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:06.700
here.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:09.640
And in fact, it was
forward thinking

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:12.520
in the view of
marrying neuroscience

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.020
and machine learning together.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:16.372
And that's why there is neuro
and there is processing.

00:16:16.372 --> 00:16:17.330
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.788
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: And that was
the goal of that conference.

00:16:19.788 --> 00:16:22.300
But once the community
started exploding

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.210
with deep learning taking
off, which is great,

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.940
but a side effect was
these conferences grew out

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:29.250
of proportion.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:33.400
There's a lot of issues of how
we are even enabling entrance

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:35.320
into these events.

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.300
And it's this
first-come-first-serve,

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:38.830
which I have a lot
of issues with.

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:43.330
So there's one issue of how to
manage the population entering

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:44.350
the conferences.

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:46.060
The other is the
behavior, right?

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:50.770
So when you have lots of
newcomers into the community,

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:54.040
there's so many people
looking for attention.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:57.940
And for them, it's this
kind of quick attention

00:16:57.940 --> 00:17:00.370
because they have
to hire talent.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:03.040
And so you have this
hedge fund that no one

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:05.700
had heard of printing
t-shirts that say,

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.700
like, "My NIPS are
[INAUDIBLE] hard."

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.000
I think that's the
very first time I saw

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:11.954
the name being used that way.

00:17:11.954 --> 00:17:12.829
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:14.859
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
And just this hedge

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:18.640
fund giving out these t-shirts
during the event, right?

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:20.260
And they got their notoriety.

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.020
They got people
to pay attention.

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.359
So I see why they had
the incentive to do that.

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:27.880
And that started increasing.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:31.180
So to me, it's this
infusion of bro culture.

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:34.720
When people feel like it's a
gold rush, it's been shown time

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.700
and again there
is a lot of people

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:38.920
who want to enter
to make a quick buck

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:42.340
or to somehow quickly
gain a profit.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:46.420
And that, to me, really has
destroyed the academic fabric

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:49.870
and the intellectual
pursuit of the conference.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:52.690
And in a way, we are all still
grappling with that change,

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:53.190
right?

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:54.430
How do we deal with it?

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:56.110
How do we manage this?

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:57.440
How do we keep it professional?

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.620
We indeed want the
community to grow.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:01.810
We indeed want
democratization of AI.

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:05.500
We wanted to reach out to
much bigger populations,

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:08.530
and we want them to
think about AI problems.

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:11.350
But how do we do it in a
way while still maintaining

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:14.020
the intellectual
exchange and while still

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.830
maintaining professionalism?

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:18.760
So to me, these
issues are on the name

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:22.570
and the way the name was
used to harass women,

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:24.820
to talk about
women's body parts,

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.400
is a reflection of the
cultural change that's

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.055
been coming into AI community.

00:18:29.055 --> 00:18:30.430
MELANIE WARRICK:
Why do you think

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.170
that these conferences
have gotten so big

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:35.672
and are so important for people
to attend now versus before?

00:18:35.672 --> 00:18:37.880
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Now it's
become a gold rush, right?

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:38.884
It's really a rush.

00:18:38.884 --> 00:18:40.300
MELANIE WARRICK:
So the gold rush?

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.300
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: In terms
of hiring the talent,

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:43.990
hiring the researchers.

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:46.240
So the companies
have done a lot.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:49.840
So a lot of amazing things of
taking AI to the real world.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:51.730
But there is also
the challenge of how

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.550
do we still maintain
the intellectual pursuit

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.850
and have avenues for
intellectual exchange,

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:01.420
and separate that from
the aspects of hiring

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:02.890
and recruiting, right?

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:05.290
And I think that many
other fields that

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.480
do these large
conferences very well,

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:10.400
there can be very
well-established protocols.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.790
But we have seen
this growth so fast,

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:16.090
and I feel that the boards
of these machine learning

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:18.760
conferences haven't
managed to adapt,

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:23.020
because I think there needs to
be some new infusion of ideas

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:23.920
there.

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.360
MELANIE WARRICK:
That makes sense.

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:28.420
So where we're at now, this year
in particular, the conference

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:33.020
took a step back and did
an assessment of the name.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:35.920
Do you know what helped
them take an assessment

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:38.170
and consider changing the name?

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:41.080
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: So like
I was saying last year

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:46.120
was, to me, like where it
kind of all topped off.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:49.450
Like a few years ago, the deep
learning revolution started,

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:53.140
and we saw all these new
people and huge crowds coming

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:54.760
to the conferences
and the character

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:56.530
of the conferences changing.

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:59.920
And then there were so many
corporate parties and events

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:02.980
and other outside
events, that became

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:04.930
almost the central
attraction compared

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:06.730
to the main conference.

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:08.710
And so when those
started multiplying,

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.490
there was no code of conduct.

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:12.580
There were no
standards established.

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:15.130
And so people, to
get attention, again,

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:18.640
were doing things that
were outside the norms

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:20.770
of professional conduct.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:23.320
The primary goal that
most researchers have,

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.870
and especially a lot of
women researchers have,

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:28.540
is to go talk about
intellectual things,

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:31.210
to talk about actual
machine learning research

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:34.511
and not worry about being
made fun of their body parts.

00:20:34.511 --> 00:20:35.510
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:38.500
And I'd heard outside
of these parties

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.890
and some additional content
that was released and published

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:44.770
around various harassment
that folks have experienced

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:46.780
at this conference
and other conferences,

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:50.110
I'd also read that there
was an open letter in March

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:53.410
from Johns Hopkins University,
that the faculty there

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:55.060
were asking the
board specifically

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:56.232
to change the name.

00:20:56.232 --> 00:20:57.940
That was something
that I had come across

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:00.752
as another component that
played into all of this.

00:21:00.752 --> 00:21:01.960
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Absolutely.

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.860
Professor Elana Fertig
was in touch with me,

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:11.020
and she was telling me of all
the efforts from Johns Hopkins.

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:14.424
And that's when I even started
plugging into this issue more.

00:21:14.424 --> 00:21:15.340
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.320
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I
was bothered by it,

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:21.340
but I realized it bothered
the younger women even much

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:23.590
more than what I
could feel, right?

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:26.800
And to me, this is
coming from an angle

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:30.660
of not something affecting
me personally as much.

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:32.850
I mean, I was angry about it.

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.420
But for others, it was
much more than that, right?

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:39.760
They literally felt like they
were not welcome in the event

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.210
or even welcome to
be an AI researcher.

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.340
And that, to me,
is catastrophic.

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:45.340
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:48.460
So the board did do
a survey this year,

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:50.570
and they came out
with their results.

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:53.246
And that was like a couple of
months back where they said,

00:21:53.246 --> 00:21:55.120
you know, we're not
going to make the change.

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.067
And they gave this
statistical analysis

00:21:57.067 --> 00:21:58.900
on why they're not going
to make the change.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:00.920
So you took a reaction to that.

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:03.610
Can you talk to that a little
bit-- about the results

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:07.094
and what steps you took
after you saw the results?

00:22:07.094 --> 00:22:09.010
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I mean,
I was, first of all,

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:12.370
disturbed that there was
a survey asking everybody

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:13.829
that there should
be a name change.

00:22:13.829 --> 00:22:14.786
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:22:14.786 --> 00:22:16.690
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: That,
to me, was already

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:20.310
a problematic starting
point, because on Twitter,

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:23.350
when I publicly supported
the name change,

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.870
and other women were
doing, there were already

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:27.280
behaviors of trolling.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:30.190
There were men writing
to me saying, oh,

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:32.080
both men and women have nipples.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.820
What's the big deal?

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:36.260
They don't even understand.

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:37.720
That's already a problem there.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:38.678
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:22:38.678 --> 00:22:40.570
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: So
the survey was already

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:45.310
an avenue for people to
debate it in an unhealthy way,

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:50.050
because this is an issue that
is not affecting the majority.

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:53.350
The majority of men,
especially senior men,

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:56.360
have never had to
worry about it.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:58.030
That's the privilege they have.

00:22:58.030 --> 00:22:59.344
And they may find it funny.

00:22:59.344 --> 00:23:00.760
They may laugh at
it for a minute.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:02.470
But that's the end of it.

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:05.650
That's the only
effect it has on them.

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:08.750
But on especially
the junior women,

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.110
the more and more
I heard on how they

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:13.840
felt so unwelcome and negative.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:16.720
And that's combined with
everything else, the parties,

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:21.430
the bro culture, the
aggressiveness in how

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:24.557
some people push their
research versus other voices

00:23:24.557 --> 00:23:25.390
are getting drowned.

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:28.240
So to me, the
reflection of all this

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:31.360
was being played out with
the name change debate.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:34.510
The majority was
being asked to make

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:38.290
a decision on an issue that
didn't affect them adversely.

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:40.870
And on the other hand,
a name change to me

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:43.390
is something so simple to do.

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:46.030
As we saw in the end,
it's not that hard.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:48.010
It's literally, go and
change on the website

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:49.272
and on social media.

00:23:49.272 --> 00:23:50.230
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:52.000
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
It costs nothing.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:54.800
And people are arguing,
oh, it's symbolic.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:57.430
But why don't we start with
at least something symbolic?

00:23:57.430 --> 00:23:58.700
Let's start there.

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:00.140
This shouldn't be the end point.

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:02.020
But if you start there,
you send a message.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:04.660
You send a message to
all the young women

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:07.780
that their voices are heard,
their concerns are heard.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:10.150
And we will do
anything to make them

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.340
more comfortable and welcome.

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:15.340
And it will also send a
message to all these bros

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:19.300
that are making fun of the
name and really destroying

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:21.220
the professional nature
of the conference

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:24.230
that this will not
be taken lightly.

00:24:24.230 --> 00:24:28.090
And hopefully over time, we can
have a strong code of conduct

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:32.530
that will actually make
this behavior accountable.

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:35.770
And so that's the path
I'm hoping this will take.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:39.670
But this is where the name
change, the way the decisions

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:42.550
were made, the way the
survey was designed,

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:45.040
and it was also a
biased sample that

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.822
was asked to take the survey.

00:24:46.822 --> 00:24:47.780
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:49.696
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: So it
was all the attendees,

00:24:49.696 --> 00:24:53.230
I believe, over the
last five years.

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:56.800
But how about so many
women who could not attend,

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:59.260
which again goes back
to my issue of access

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:00.880
to these conferences.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:02.680
If it's like a
first-come-first-serve,

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:05.710
if you're comparing
this to the Burning Man,

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:09.760
if you are comparing this to
these hot tickets getting sold

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:14.110
out, think about young
women who may, first of all,

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:17.770
be not so confident if they
even want to go to conference,

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.790
or if they have challenges in
getting the travel support.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:24.130
Both men and women in
the junior positions

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:27.540
who are even thinking about
it but haven't managed,

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.135
they will be the ones who
will lose out the most.

00:25:30.135 --> 00:25:30.390
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:32.056
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: And
those in academia,

00:25:32.056 --> 00:25:34.230
because they may not
have the same resources

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:35.950
that they have in industry.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:38.730
So this has been an
extremely biased sample

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:41.790
of both men and women who
were asked to take the survey.

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:44.760
And my view is, it was
not affecting them.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.070
It was affecting
more people who are

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:48.420
about to enter the community.

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.090
The more junior people,
they are much more

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.650
adversely affected than the
senior people who are well

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:54.967
established in the community.

00:25:54.967 --> 00:25:56.550
MELANIE WARRICK: I
also think I've yet

00:25:56.550 --> 00:25:58.674
to meet someone who's never
heard of the conference

00:25:58.674 --> 00:26:00.727
before, that, when you
tell them the name,

00:26:00.727 --> 00:26:02.310
they don't immediately
think that it's

00:26:02.310 --> 00:26:04.560
an inappropriate
acronym to be using.

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:06.660
But when the results
were given, you

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:08.570
responded by
starting a petition.

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:12.450
You specifically started out
with the protestNIPS hashtag,

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:15.720
and then also created a
petition on change.org.

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.600
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I should admit
I was really late to the party.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:20.910
There was a lot
of hard work done

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:23.580
at the grassroots level, a
lot of women speaking out

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:24.690
on Twitter.

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:29.040
And as I said, from Johns
Hopkins University, Dr. Elana

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:33.330
Fertig and so many others, they
coordinated with the Title IX

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:35.490
office there,
drafted this letter,

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:37.740
got all these
researchers to sign this.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:40.990
So they've done so much
groundwork to begin with.

00:26:40.990 --> 00:26:43.630
And when I came
in, I was thinking,

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:45.990
let's take this broader, right?

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:48.240
Let's kind of open up
to not just machine

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:50.580
learning people,
everybody on Twitter.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.040
Because the general
population will

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:56.910
be so shocked to see this
acronym is a real thing, right?

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:00.030
And that's where I tried
to de-bias the samples,

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:03.720
because not just the
people who have already

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:05.400
been in the community
and take this name

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:11.070
for granted, but fresh voices
that have a real issue with it.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:14.230
And I really wanted to
see the board take notice.

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:17.760
So I essentially repurposed
the letter from Johns Hopkins

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.280
and put that on
change.org to reach out

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:21.951
to the broader community.

00:27:21.951 --> 00:27:23.200
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:26.460
So then what happened
after this petition?

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:29.280
You've already talked to
how people have rebelled

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:31.860
against the name change,
but what kind of reaction

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.170
did you get when you started
publicly speaking out

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:35.550
against this?

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:38.990
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I got a lot
of support that I'm so happy,

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.940
and I find that
very fulfilling how

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:44.490
the community came
behind me, and so

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:47.070
many men and women retweeting.

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:49.890
Jeff Dean immediately
retweeted, and he

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:54.900
wanted to think of getting the
companies to be accountable

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.050
and supporting this
name change, right?

00:27:58.050 --> 00:28:00.600
So that to me really
amplified the voice.

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:04.350
So there were so many
amazing researchers

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:07.159
who really helped amplify this.

00:28:07.159 --> 00:28:08.075
MELANIE WARRICK: Good.

00:28:08.075 --> 00:28:10.260
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: And that
was the majority, right?

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:14.160
So like the majority are
amazing, good-hearted people.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.170
And then there was
a small section

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:18.810
who were genuinely questioning.

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:21.690
And they were really confused.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:24.810
Many of them were
not native speakers.

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:28.470
They kind of didn't
know the short form,

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:32.550
or they didn't kind of
really get this immediately.

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:34.650
So that's why I had
put the Google image

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:36.612
search to even begin with that.

00:28:36.612 --> 00:28:38.070
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's great impact.

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:40.560
Visuals are wonderful
for these things, yes.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:42.510
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: So
that smaller section

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:45.150
of people who were
openly speaking out.

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:46.800
They spoke with
their real identities

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:49.350
but had genuine questions.

00:28:49.350 --> 00:28:50.880
Most of them came around.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:52.950
I would say most of
them at least realized,

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:56.100
yes, it may not have
affected them, and that's OK.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:58.620
But it's affected
so many others.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:01.500
So they saw all
these voices that

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:04.060
were saying that they
were adversely affected.

00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:06.780
And then they realized
they need to support this.

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:08.860
And so that, to me, was
the best part of it,

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:12.990
because how many people who
are in this neutral or moderate

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:16.890
position, who just are not even
aware of the issues affecting

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:17.820
the minorities?

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:20.340
But when we bring
this out into the open

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:23.370
and have a dialogue,
that we can really

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:27.960
get this information exchange,
and we can increase empathy.

00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:31.420
And that was the most
fulfilling part of it.

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:33.510
But there was also
the negative side.

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:36.120
There was a lot of trolling.

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:37.620
No surprise.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:39.660
All of it was anonymous.

00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:42.000
And then there was
the Reddit thread

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:45.420
that personally
was abusive to me

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:48.900
in so many appallingly
distasteful ways.

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:52.260
I got a lot of the accounts
that I now had to block.

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:56.490
I really had to keep pushing
Reddit to remove those.

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:59.600
And it took extensive
ones, and still there

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:01.110
is some that are still there.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:06.510
So this brings out the concern
that anybody having a voice

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:09.390
pushing for inclusivity
or diversity

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:12.150
is heavily trolled
on social media.

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:14.610
And we have so
little control on how

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:17.580
to avoid this hateful speech.

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:19.830
And that to me is
really disturbing

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.940
because it prevents so many
women from speaking up.

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.280
MELANIE WARRICK: It's true.

00:30:24.280 --> 00:30:27.210
I'm hesitant to ask, but
partially want to ask.

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:29.820
How bad was the
trolling for you?

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.302
Are you willing to talk a
little bit about how bad it got?

00:30:32.302 --> 00:30:33.260
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Sure.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:37.050
And in my Twitter thread, I did
post some of those screenshots

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:40.980
because I wanted people to
be aware of how bad it is.

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:44.280
So many men were shocked because
they've never seen anything

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:45.850
like that happen to them.

00:30:45.850 --> 00:30:47.670
They've never been
personally subjected

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:50.760
to such kind of
harassment and trolling.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:53.370
It was commenting on my looks.

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:59.780
It was commenting on
my professionalism.

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:02.730
It was saying that I just
got up to this position

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:04.770
because I'm a
woman, and how can I

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:08.470
be so not thankful to all
the men who have helped me.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:09.300
It was all this.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.610
They called me a
radical feminist.

00:31:11.610 --> 00:31:14.160
And I know another
woman who went on Reddit

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:16.920
and tried to answer
to these trolls.

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:18.120
She was bullied.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.790
She was physically
threatened with a gun.

00:31:20.790 --> 00:31:23.300
And this is a professor
who is a public figure.

00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:28.470
And then you really start
to wonder how dangerous this

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:29.730
could all get.

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:33.060
And then the worst
thing was not just

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:36.750
the personal trolling
that I was receiving,

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:40.480
but how other young women
were bullied through all this.

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:44.880
And I was receiving emails from
so many young women saying--

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:46.420
and also young men--

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:49.500
saying that when they were
discussing it with others,

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:51.730
that I was just being trolled.

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:54.150
I was kind of looked down upon.

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:57.450
And sometimes the advisors
would tell their students

00:31:57.450 --> 00:31:59.580
not to sign the
petitions publicly

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:02.490
or never to lend their
voice to this even

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:03.930
though they wanted to.

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:07.140
So you could say, oh, this
is a harmless name change.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:10.912
But it opened up this underbelly
of harassment and bullying.

00:32:10.912 --> 00:32:12.120
MELANIE WARRICK: Viciousness.

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:13.036
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Yes.

00:32:13.036 --> 00:32:15.630
There's so much-- that's
the word, viciousness.

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:18.040
If it was so simple
and easy to do,

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:20.507
then why are people behaving
so viciously about it?

00:32:20.507 --> 00:32:21.840
MELANIE WARRICK: It's atrocious.

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:24.570
And there's not really
enough right words,

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:27.960
but something that's in my head
right now that I need to say

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:29.620
is thank you.

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:33.180
Thank you for fighting
for the change.

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:35.910
In addition to the others
who have done that,

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:38.700
thank you for putting
the energy to that,

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:41.480
because this isn't your job.

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:42.750
This shouldn't be your job.

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:44.310
It shouldn't be anyone's job.

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:47.460
And thank you for continuing
to be a leader in the field

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.776
and not leaving
because of the things

00:32:49.776 --> 00:32:51.150
that you've seen
and experienced.

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:51.749
So thank you.

00:32:51.749 --> 00:32:53.290
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
Thank you, Melanie.

00:32:53.290 --> 00:32:54.960
Thank you for
amplifying the voice,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:56.710
and thank you for
all the support

00:32:56.710 --> 00:33:00.420
when, as all this was unfolding,
you were messaging me.

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:03.800
You were being such a
great source of support.

00:33:03.800 --> 00:33:07.590
And that's where I found
all these amazing allies.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:11.130
We grew stronger that
way, and we know next time

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:14.510
to do more meaningful
changes, who the people are.

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:16.410
Now I know my community.

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:18.150
Now I know the allies.

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:20.040
And that's so important.

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:23.654
And yes, I'm here to stay,
trolling or otherwise.

00:33:23.654 --> 00:33:24.570
MELANIE WARRICK: Good.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:26.490
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
That's not going to--

00:33:26.490 --> 00:33:28.490
that's only going
to make me stronger.

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:32.310
And that'll only-- you know, the
days of trolling are numbered.

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.510
I would really like
to see big changes,

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:36.270
and I would like
to do everything

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:39.030
that I can to not
only limit trolling,

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:43.410
but to limit all these kinds of
bad behavior in the community

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:44.870
and in the wider space.

00:33:44.870 --> 00:33:45.870
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:48.060
Transitioning back,
you had the petition,

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:51.570
and between doing the petition
and all the things that you've

00:33:51.570 --> 00:33:54.270
been through and the support
that you've received,

00:33:54.270 --> 00:33:56.220
it seems like the
board has reconsidered,

00:33:56.220 --> 00:33:59.490
because just recently
before the conference,

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:01.650
they released a change.

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.930
And the change is
somewhat of a compromise.

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:05.924
Instead of changing
the full name,

00:34:05.924 --> 00:34:07.590
they're now changing--
you know, they're

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:09.810
keeping Neural Information
Processing Systems,

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:12.130
but the acronym is
going to be NeurIPS.

00:34:12.130 --> 00:34:16.150
So kind of like stirrups,
but not exactly.

00:34:16.150 --> 00:34:17.821
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
That's not the picture

00:34:17.821 --> 00:34:19.942
I would like in my mind, but--

00:34:19.942 --> 00:34:21.150
MELANIE WARRICK: What's that?

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:23.233
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I said,
I don't want the picture

00:34:23.233 --> 00:34:24.670
of stirrups in my mind.

00:34:24.670 --> 00:34:25.670
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:27.690
It's that word that
keeps coming into my head

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:30.389
every time I hear people
say the new acronym.

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:33.812
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Maybe the
[? turnips ?] is a more--

00:34:33.812 --> 00:34:34.770
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:37.500
So what do you think
of the new name?

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:40.409
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I think the
new name is a nice compromise.

00:34:40.409 --> 00:34:42.750
I would have liked to
start with a clean slate,

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:44.710
because that wouldn't
have been any harder.

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:50.190
But I mean, I'm happy that
we came up to this point.

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:53.130
And as I said, it's much
more about the name.

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:57.030
It's all the forces
that came into the open

00:34:57.030 --> 00:35:00.550
when we were debating
this name change.

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:04.050
That's the one we need
to work a lot more on.

00:35:04.050 --> 00:35:06.660
So I'd really like to
move to the next steps.

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:11.220
I would really like to see
a much more impactful code

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:12.390
of conduct.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.050
The current one
has been amazing.

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:16.110
It was done in a
short amount of time.

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:19.590
Hal Daumé and Katherine Heller
just did an amazing job to put

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:20.670
that together.

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:22.950
But now I want to ask,
what are the repercussions

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:25.140
if somebody does not meet that?

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:27.450
There has to be
real repercussions,

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:29.910
and only then it
should have teeth.

00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:33.000
So these are the things
we need to ask ourselves

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.104
as the next steps.

00:35:34.104 --> 00:35:35.520
MELANIE WARRICK:
Is there anything

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:36.936
that you'd recommend
for those who

00:35:36.936 --> 00:35:40.440
might want to help promote
inclusivity in the field?

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:42.870
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Have a
professional interaction

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:46.260
and be very mindful if
you're talking to people

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:48.690
who are more junior to you.

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:50.760
Even the actions you
don't even think about

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:52.780
may have a big impact on them.

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:54.450
And communicate better.

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:56.730
See if people are
feeling comfortable

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:58.240
when you're talking to them.

00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:00.390
So I would start
with those basics.

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:02.940
I think Jeff Dean had an
amazing Twitter thread where

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:05.640
he crowdsourced experiences
of women researchers

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:09.060
and conferences and negative
experiences they had.

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:12.300
And then he said, oh, these are
the things you shouldn't do.

00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:15.390
For instance, in the very
first conference I went to,

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:16.800
my advisor is great.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:19.860
He introduced me to another
senior male researcher,

00:36:19.860 --> 00:36:21.000
a professor.

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.160
And he left, you know,
so he introduced me.

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:25.210
He had to go talk
to somebody else.

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:26.970
He left, and he
said, go further,

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:29.070
take over this conversation.

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:32.250
And then that professor told
me, oh, let me introduce you

00:36:32.250 --> 00:36:34.290
to my female graduate student.

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:37.740
Because you both are women, you
will get along better with her.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:40.114
I mean, like that, to me,
was just like-- you know,

00:36:40.114 --> 00:36:41.490
I'm a starting researcher.

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:43.770
I really would like to
talk to this senior person

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:44.980
that I looked up to.

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:47.520
And this is just--

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:52.860
for him it seems so natural, but
it had such a bad impact on me.

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:56.730
And it's important for women
to express all these kinds

00:36:56.730 --> 00:37:00.960
of behaviors that made them
feel uncomfortable, unwelcome,

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:05.790
diminished, and then for men
to think about taking notice,

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:08.400
and then to open
this up even broader

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:10.120
to other diversity groups.

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:16.050
I know Black in AI has seen
amazing effort into diversity.

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:18.480
I mean, we had
researchers coming

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.850
from countries that
never had anybody attend

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:22.440
these conferences before.

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:25.930
That to me shows there's so
much work to be done there.

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:28.260
But they've also
received heavy trolling,

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:31.300
undergoing a lot of issues.

00:37:31.300 --> 00:37:34.200
So to me, yes, we
start with women now,

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:36.400
because I can mirror
those experiences.

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:37.150
I understand them.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:40.690
But I want to understand how is
it impacting the other groups?

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:42.600
What are the issues
the others are

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:44.890
facing that I may never have?

00:37:44.890 --> 00:37:47.430
I have my own
privileges, but others

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:51.340
come from different backgrounds
and are facing more issues.

00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:53.254
How can we address
everybody's issues?

00:37:53.254 --> 00:37:55.170
MELANIE WARRICK: I think
that's a great point.

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.870
And we are releasing
this podcast the week

00:37:57.870 --> 00:38:00.750
before the conference,
the NeurIPS conference.

00:38:00.750 --> 00:38:02.250
And the workshops,
as you mentioned,

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:05.190
like Black in AI
and LatinX and WIML.

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:07.470
So now that the name
has been changed,

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:09.400
there has been the
compromise, what

00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:11.650
are you looking forward to
in terms of the conference?

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:15.220
What are some of the areas in
which you're excited about,

00:38:15.220 --> 00:38:17.600
that you think are
great to keep an eye on,

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:20.160
outside of that diversity
efforts that are going on

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:22.972
to help bring in more
researchers into this space?

00:38:22.972 --> 00:38:24.180
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: Absolutely.

00:38:24.180 --> 00:38:27.060
To me, going to
these conferences

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:30.730
has always been about
the new innovations.

00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:35.400
Like what are the new technical
breakthroughs, and also

00:38:35.400 --> 00:38:38.940
so much of what young people are
doing, like young researchers,

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:42.150
like how we can further
nurture and encourage them?

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:44.610
If they are coming for the
conference for the first time

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:47.670
or presenting the
paper, how can I

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:51.020
take that to the next level,
how I can communicate with them

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:52.410
on all the broader pictures?

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:55.860
So that's where I get the
most out of these conferences.

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:58.200
And in the workshops,
a lot more exchange

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:01.230
can happen because
it's much more organic.

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:04.740
It's much more work that
has just started or is

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:06.840
in preliminary stages, right?

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:10.590
So there is a lot more
room for brainstorming,

00:39:10.590 --> 00:39:13.410
for giving feedback,
for receiving feedback.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:16.890
In the past I've had just
so many results that derive

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:18.010
during the conference.

00:39:18.010 --> 00:39:20.730
I'd just sit,
brainstorm with people,

00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:23.250
and come up with new ideas.

00:39:23.250 --> 00:39:26.820
And now, as the community has
scaled, that has become harder.

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:30.190
But I would like to still
pursue some of this.

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:32.700
And this is where we need
to control the crowds,

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:36.300
and we need to find ways that
make this more manageable.

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:39.380
In fact, I tend to do
this more at WIML or Black

00:39:39.380 --> 00:39:41.310
in AI because they're
smaller events,

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:45.100
and I can talk at a personal
level and exchange ideas.

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:46.350
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:39:46.350 --> 00:39:48.690
Two last questions,
one being, you've

00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:49.886
done a lot of interviews.

00:39:49.886 --> 00:39:52.260
Any questions that nobody asks
you that you wish somebody

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:53.190
would ask?

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:54.990
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR:
So people have asked

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:57.330
me a lot about industry
versus academia

00:39:57.330 --> 00:40:00.270
because I'm in both sides.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:03.990
And I see the good and
bad in each of them.

00:40:03.990 --> 00:40:05.730
And so most of the
questions typically

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:08.320
are about how industry
is snatching away

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:09.570
the academic talent.

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:12.790
How can we avoid
it or change that?

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:14.910
And for that I always
say, look, there

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:18.300
are lots of positive impacts,
because many professors

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:20.550
like me are dividing time
between both industry

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:25.080
and academia and really
helping in this transfer

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:30.150
of information, not only from
academia to industry, but back.

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:33.690
So the question that nobody
has ever asked me before

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:37.590
is how is industry impacting
academia in a positive way,

00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:40.350
and what are still
unrealized [INAUDIBLE]??

00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:43.350
Because there are also
lots of frustrations

00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:45.720
to me and to other researchers
with the current academic

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:46.770
system.

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:49.260
And that, to me,
is the way research

00:40:49.260 --> 00:40:51.690
is so much focused
on publications,

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:56.130
but also so little focused on
creating software frameworks,

00:40:56.130 --> 00:40:59.430
creating a community effort
where people can really

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:05.280
build an ecosystem and think
about modular softwares that

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:08.790
will benefit others, not
only that they have done

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:10.530
some research,
done their own code

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:14.880
and they are writing this
paper and publishing this.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:17.610
And that's, to me, industry
has changed this game,

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:21.150
because the impact is coming
from having these open source

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:23.820
frameworks, and the
community is using it

00:41:23.820 --> 00:41:25.600
and contributing to this.

00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:27.960
And so what I really hope
for, and what I'm pushing for,

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:31.320
is how can we do this in
other disciplines of sciences?

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:33.850
The problem is, in the
other basic sciences,

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:35.640
there is no industry
doing this now.

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:38.260
No industry is doing
any research on this.

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:40.680
So what about all
of these other areas

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:43.370
that are not
disrupted by industry?

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:46.020
I would like to see
disruption, disruption

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:49.080
in the sense of how
the incentives are

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:50.580
aligned in better ways.

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:53.400
How we can really
remove these barriers

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:56.880
that are mostly
sociological, because there

00:41:56.880 --> 00:41:58.770
was this very old
legacy code that

00:41:58.770 --> 00:42:01.380
was used in some high-energy
physics, where nobody even

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:03.110
understands what's going on.

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:04.470
It's still being used today.

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:06.900
Even when we have
billions of dollars

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:09.950
of experiments being done,
the experimental part

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:12.190
takes so much resources.

00:42:12.190 --> 00:42:14.310
And that's where the
grants go to funding them.

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:17.400
But on the computational
side, ultimately

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:20.130
to enable AI into
those applications,

00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:22.410
there are basic barriers.

00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:24.960
They may seem sociological
and more mundane--

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:27.270
they're not a
mathematical barrier--

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:29.220
but they are important barriers.

00:42:29.220 --> 00:42:33.161
And I don't have a good
answer on how to disrupt that.

00:42:33.161 --> 00:42:35.160
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
a good question, though.

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:38.389
Anything else that you wanted
to touch on before I let you go?

00:42:38.389 --> 00:42:40.680
ANIMA ANANDKUMAR: I wanted
to thank you again, Melanie.

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:46.530
It's been wonderful that you
have asked so many questions

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:50.640
where I could really express
the emotions I was going through

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:53.700
as I went through
this protestNIPS

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:57.150
and be engaged
with the community.

00:42:57.150 --> 00:43:01.020
I really appreciate you
amplifying this voice

00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:02.739
and lending support.

00:43:02.739 --> 00:43:04.530
I'm really thankful
for this, and thank you

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:06.080
for doing this podcast.

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:07.500
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, thank
you for coming and sharing

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:08.460
the story with us.

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:10.130
I really appreciate it, Anima.

00:43:10.130 --> 00:43:11.730
OK, question of the week.

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:14.580
If you are dealing with any
type of bullying or trolling

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:16.740
or you're concerned
about this, what

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.480
are some things you can do?

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:21.240
Well, I've included in
here a couple of links

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:22.860
that you can check out.

00:43:22.860 --> 00:43:27.750
There is some great information
from the Electronic Frontiers

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:31.500
Foundation for surveillance,
self-defense, tips and tools.

00:43:31.500 --> 00:43:34.150
I would not recommend reading
it all in one sitting.

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:36.360
I would say go in
there, look around, pick

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:38.520
and choose what makes sense.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:41.160
And also Feminist
Frequency has provided

00:43:41.160 --> 00:43:43.230
some wonderful information
to help step you

00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:46.140
through ways to better
protect yourself online.

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:47.610
This isn't a foolproof process.

00:43:47.610 --> 00:43:49.332
This isn't a perfect process.

00:43:49.332 --> 00:43:50.790
But at least it's
something you can

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:53.455
do if you are interested
in taking certain steps.

00:43:53.455 --> 00:43:55.080
And they're not the
only ones out there

00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:57.480
who have provided content,
so you can definitely

00:43:57.480 --> 00:43:59.520
do some searching
to explore further.

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:01.710
All right, so where
are we all going to be?

00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:03.870
Well, as we've been
talking about it,

00:44:03.870 --> 00:44:06.220
Mark will be at
KubeCon in December.

00:44:06.220 --> 00:44:08.850
So if you're going to be
there, KubeCon, Koob-Con,

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:12.240
however you pronounce it, you
should go and say hello to him.

00:44:12.240 --> 00:44:14.460
And then I will be at
SOCML actually this week.

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:17.550
And next week, as I
mentioned, will be NeurIPS.

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:19.480
If you're going to be
in the area of Montreal,

00:44:19.480 --> 00:44:21.800
you should definitely check
out some of the workshops.

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:25.920
WIML is going to be on
December 3, and Black in AI

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:28.410
is going to be on
December 7, and LatinX

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:29.580
will be on December 8.

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:31.177
So if you're there, go by.

00:44:31.177 --> 00:44:32.760
Check out these
different communities.

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:34.801
They're good ones to be
learning from, especially

00:44:34.801 --> 00:44:35.890
in the AI space.

00:44:35.890 --> 00:44:38.250
That's it for us for
this week on the podcast.

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:41.460
So thank you for joining us,
and we'll talk to you next week.

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:43.910
